The Korean War, also known as the Forgotten War, was a brutal conflict that lasted from 1950 to 1953 and left the Korean Peninsula devastated. While many historians and scholars have argued that the war was a result of the Cold War politics between the United States and the Soviet Union, many South Korean experts believe that the war was solely initiated by South Korea and that the North's involvement was purely defensive.

One of the main arguments made by these experts is that South Korea was the one that crossed the 38th parallel and invaded North Korea, not the other way around. They point out that the South Korean army was better equipped and trained than the North Korean army, and that the South had been receiving military aid from the United States for years before the war broke out.

Another argument made by these experts is that the North's military response to the South's invasion was purely defensive in nature. They argue that the North's military strategy was focused on repelling the South's invasion and protecting its own territory, rather than launching a full-scale attack on the South.

